Social Media App — Project Plan
A learning project: microservices-based social app with posts, follows, and messaging
1. Project Overview
A social media style application (similar in spirit to Facebook/Instagram) built primarily as a backend learning project. The backend is built independently using Java, Spring Boot, and a microservices architecture, covering API Gateway, load balancing, service discovery, and inter-service communication. The frontend will be built separately with AI assistance.
2. Minimum Viable Features (Version 1)
User
Sign up and login
Profile with name, unique username, photo, bio, and location
No email verification or password reset in v1
Posts
Image upload with caption
Like count and comment count
Friends / Connections
Follow and unfollow — no approval flow required
Messaging
One-to-one text messaging only (no group messaging in v1)
3. Step-by-Step Development Process
Define Scope — Lock down exact features and explicitly note what is out of scope for version 1.
Design the Data Model — Sketch core entities and their relationships before writing any code.
Define Service Boundaries — Decide which entities belong to which microservice.
Design APIs — Define REST contracts for each service before implementation.
Pick Infrastructure Pieces — Choose API Gateway, service discovery (e.g. Eureka), and config server.
Inter-Service Communication — Decide where to use synchronous calls (REST/WebClient) vs asynchronous messaging (Kafka/RabbitMQ).
Database Strategy — Each microservice gets its own database; plan cross-service consistency (e.g. Saga pattern).
Security — Set up JWT-based authentication/authorization, either as its own auth service or at the gateway.
Build One Vertical Slice First — Fully implement the simplest service end-to-end (DB → API → gateway routing) before replicating the pattern.
Containerize with Docker — Each service and its dependencies run consistently in containers.
Orchestration & Deployment — Use Docker Compose locally, or Kubernetes for further learning.
Observability — Add logging, metrics, and tracing across services.
Resilience Patterns — Add circuit breakers and retries (e.g. Resilience4j).
Frontend Integration — Connect the AI-assisted frontend to the API Gateway.
Testing & Iteration — Test end-to-end flows and iterate on features.
4. Relevant Design Patterns
Architecture-Level Patterns
API Gateway — single entry point for all client requests
Service Discovery (e.g. Eureka) — dynamic lookup of service instances
Circuit Breaker (Resilience4j) — prevents cascading failures
Saga Pattern — manages distributed transactions across services
Backend for Frontend — optional, tailored API layer for the frontend
Service-Level (OOP) Patterns
Repository Pattern — data access layer (largely provided by Spring Data JPA)
Factory Pattern — useful for creating different notification/post types
Observer Pattern — notifying followers when a user posts
Strategy Pattern — swappable logic, e.g. feed ranking algorithms
Messaging Patterns
Event-Driven Architecture
Publish/Subscribe — via Kafka or RabbitMQ
